{"builder":"Make one pull request that meets every acceptance line of issue #23 (clamp() for mathlib).\n\nContext: the repository root has math.js (exports add, subtract, multiply via module.exports), README.md (an \"## API\" section with one \"### name(args)\" heading per function, but today it only covers add and multiply, so subtract is missing), test/math.test.js, and package.json whose test script is `node --test`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In math.js add a function clamp(x, lo, hi) with a short doc comment, following the style of the existing functions. If lo > hi, throw a RangeError (e.g. `throw new RangeError('clamp: lo must not be greater than hi')`). Otherwise return x limited to the range: lo if x < lo, hi if x > hi, else x (e.g. Math.min(Math.max(x, lo), hi)). Add clamp to module.exports alongside add, subtract and multiply. Keep the existing exports unchanged.\n2. In test/math.test.js add tests for clamp using the same test style already in that file: a value below the range returns lo (e.g. clamp(-5, 0, 100) === 0), a value above returns hi (clamp(140, 0, 100) === 100), a value inside is returned unchanged (clamp(42, 0, 100) === 42), boundary values (clamp(0, 0, 100) === 0, clamp(100, 0, 100) === 100), lo === hi works (clamp(7, 5, 5) === 5), and lo > hi throws a RangeError (assert.throws(() => clamp(5, 10, 0), RangeError)).\n3. In README.md, under the API section, add a \"### clamp(x, lo, hi)\" entry describing that it returns x limited to lo..hi and throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi, with a short example. Also add a \"### subtract(a, b)\" entry (returns a minus b, with an example), because README.md must document every function math.js exports and subtract is currently undocumented. Afterwards, compare the keys of `require('./math.js')` against the README headings and make sure each exported name has its own entry.\n4. Run `npm test` from the repository root and make sure it passes with no failing tests. Also run `node -e \"const m=require('./math.js'); console.log(Object.keys(m), m.clamp(-5,0,100), m.clamp(140,0,100), m.clamp(42,0,100))\"` and confirm the output is `[ 'add', 'subtract', 'multiply', 'clamp' ] 0 100 42`.\n5. Commit the changes to math.js, test/math.test.js and README.md and open the pull request, referencing https://github.com/o/r/issues/23 in its description.","verifier":"Check the pull request for issue #23 at its head. Do every check from the repository root at the PR head, and run things; reading the diff alone is not enough. Give one verdict per check, in this order.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"math.js exports clamp(x, lo, hi), which returns x limited to the range lo to hi\".\nRun: `node -e \"const m=require('./math.js'); console.log(typeof m.clamp, m.clamp(-5,0,100), m.clamp(140,0,100), m.clamp(42,0,100), m.clamp(0,0,100), m.clamp(100,0,100), m.clamp(7,5,5), m.clamp(-3,-10,-1), m.clamp(2.5,0,1))\"`.\nPASS: clamp is exported as a function, called with arguments in the order (x, lo, hi), and the printed values are `function 0 100 42 0 100 5 -1 1` (values below the range give lo, above give hi, inside or on the edges give x).\nFAIL: clamp is not exported, is not a function, throws on any of these valid calls, or any printed value differs from the above.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"clamp(x, lo, hi) throws a RangeError when lo is greater than hi\".\nRun: `node -e \"const {clamp}=require('./math.js'); for (const a of [[5,10,0],[0,1,-1],[50,100,0]]) { try { clamp(...a); console.log('no throw', a); } catch (e) { console.log(e instanceof RangeError ? 'RangeError' : 'other: '+e, a); } }\"`.\nPASS: each of the three calls prints `RangeError`.\nFAIL: any call returns without throwing, or throws something that is not a RangeError.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"README.md documents every function math.js exports\".\nRun: `node -e \"console.log(Object.keys(require('./math.js')).filter(k => typeof require('./math.js')[k]==='function'))\"` to list the exported functions, then read README.md at the head.\nPASS: for every exported function name listed (this should include add, subtract, multiply and clamp), README.md contains documentation of that function: its name with its signature or a description of what it does.\nFAIL: any exported function is not documented in README.md (note: subtract was undocumented before this change, so check it specifically).\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"npm test passes\".\nRun: `npm test` from the repository root.\nPASS: the command finishes and reports the tests passing (no failing tests).\nFAIL: the command reports any failing test, or errors out.\n\nAdvisory (not part of any PASS or FAIL above):\n- `npm test` should exit with status 0, and it is good if test/math.test.js includes tests for clamp, including the RangeError case.\n- A clear RangeError message (e.g. naming lo and hi) is nice to have.\n- Mutation idea: temporarily change clamp to drop the lo > hi check and confirm a test fails; revert afterwards and do not commit it.\n- Existing exports add, subtract and multiply should still behave as before."}